| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Nagle | [100, 300, 200] | 600 | 600 | 0 | 0 | 120 |
| immediate | [100, 100, 100, 100, 100, 100] | 600 | 600 | 0 | 0 | 240 |
\image(figures/nagle_drained.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Header arithmetic after both models send and acknowledge the same600-byte payload.)
\alttext(Three versus six packets produce120 versus240 assumed header bytes; final ACKs are explicit inputs.)

> Full packets bypass the partial-write hold

With MSS500, write100 then1000 without an ACK. The first write emits100; the
second emits two full500-byte packets despite the existing outstanding data. The
actual result is1100 sent bytes, no unsent buffer and1100 outstanding. This is a
packetization rule, not permission to ignore a real TCP congestion or receive
window.

MSS must be strictly positive. Every event kind and nonnegative byte count is
validated before simulation, correcting the legacy zero-MSS infinite loop and
negative-byte behavior. Widened cumulative byte counters throw on overflow.
Ordinary work is O(events+emitted packets), with constant sender state plus
packet output. Optional history owns the input, actual event states and packet
causes, with2+2*events+2*packets logical units; default1000000, zero disables.
Actual quota failures return no partial result.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/nagle_full.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t =
        dm::algorithms::nagle_trace({{dm::algorithms::NagleEvent::Write, 100},
                                     {dm::algorithms::NagleEvent::Write, 1000}},
                                    500);
    std::vector<double> payloads;
    std::vector<std::string> labels;
    std::print("| actual packet | triggering event | payload bytes | actual reason code |\n| ---: | ---: | " "---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.emissions.size(); ++i) {
        auto &e = t.emissions[i];
        payloads.push_back(e.bytes);
        labels.push_back(std::to_string(i));
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", i, e.event, e.bytes, e.reason);
    }
    std::print("\nFinal sent {}, buffered {}, outstanding {}. Capture units {}.\n",
               t.result.sent, t.result.buffered, t.result.unacked,
               t.captured_entries);
    auto p =
        dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(payloads, labels, "actual payload bytes");
    p.title("Full-segment bypass with existing outstanding data").x_label(
        "actual packet index").y_label("payload bytes").save_svg(
        "build/nagle_full.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual packet | triggering event | payload bytes | actual reason code |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0 | 100 | 1 |
| 1 | 1 | 500 | 0 |
| 2 | 1 | 500 | 0 |

Final sent 1100, buffered 0, outstanding 1100. Capture units 12.
\image(figures/nagle_full.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual full-segment bypass in the ordinal model.)
\alttext(Two500-byte payloads are emitted while an earlier100-byte packet remains outstanding.)

> Conclusion and references

The state histories explain coalescing and release while conserving every byte.
The retained original stream ends with an unsent tail; an explicitly drained
variant provides the equal-payload header comparison. Full-segment bypass,
complete input guards and the absence of time/window simulation define what
these examples can establish.

[Nagle, Congestion Control in IP/TCP Internetworks, RFC896 (1984)|https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc896.html]
supplies the small-packet suppression context. The library executes the stated
ordinal sender simplification, not a full TCP stack.

